<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Louis, Count of Nassau-Dillenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a>–<a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Louis was Count of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> and stadtholder of Friesland from 1584, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>. With his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> he reformed the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, introducing regular drill and the countermarch for continuous volley fire, inspired by Roman models. He commanded in Friesland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> during the Eighty Years' War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-louis-count-of-nassau-dillenburg.jpg" alt="William Louis, Count of Nassau-Dillenburg" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willem_Lodewijk_van_Nassau_1560-1620.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maurice of Nassau, stadtholder from 1585, and his cousin <b>William Louis</b> reformed the army with regular pay, smaller units, constant drill and volley fire, and with the engineer Simon Stevin they recaptured towns such as Breda in 1590 and won the Battle of Nieuwpoort in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1590s Maurice of Nassau and his cousin <b>William Louis</b>, commanding the forces of the Dutch Republic in its war against Spain, studied the military manuals of classical antiquity and drilled their musketeers to fire by ranks: each rank fired and then withdrew to reload while the…</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeuwarden">Leeuwarden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.73,8.28&amp;year=1599" aria-label="Dillenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4739 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Louis, Count of Nassau-Dillenburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5189 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5189 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.73,8.28&amp;year=1599">What was here in 1599? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
